{"state": {"case_id": "MB_13", "timestep": 2, "case_summary": "A 55-year-old man presents to his primary care physician with fatigue, malaise, and a painful rash. The patient states that his symptoms have been worsening over the past week. He also has experienced abdominal pain and diarrhea. He has a medical history of obesity and smokes regularly. His temperature is 97.5°F (36.4°C), blood pressure is 142/82 mmHg, pulse is 85/min, respirations are 15/min, and oxygen saturation is 98% on room air. Physical exam is notable for an erythematous rash with papules and plaques on the patient's face, torso, and extremities. He states that the rash is painful. A fingerstick blood glucose is unable to accurately read his blood glucose and gives a reading of > 500 mg/dL. Which of the following is the most likely underlying pathophysiology of this patient’s condition?\n\nOptions:\nA. Alpha cell tumor\nB. Beta cell destruction\nC. Beta cell tumor\nD. Hypercortisolism\nE. Insulin resistance\n", "root": {"label": "Subacute Multisystem Syndrome with Hyperglycemia and Painful Rash of Uncertain Aetiology", "time_course": "subacute", "severity": "unspecified", "confidence": 0.4, "supporting_facts": ["Painful erythematous rash with papules and plaques on face, torso, and extremities", "Fingerstick blood glucose >500 mg/dL (Tier 2 evidence)", "Constitutional symptoms (fatigue, malaise) and digestive tract involvement (abdominal pain, diarrhea)", "Worsening symptoms over 1 week"], "excluded_candidates": ["Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA): lacks classic signs of dehydration/metabolic acidosis", "Varicella zoster infection (shingles): rash distribution not described as unilateral dermatomal", "Drug eruption: no medication history provided, but hyperglycemia is primary finding", "Systemic infection: temperature is subnormal (97.5°F) rather than febrile"], "alarm_features": ["Severe hyperglycemia (>500 mg/dL)", "Multisystem involvement (skin, GI, constitutional)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1"